EXT. SALT FLAT - DAY

White to the horizon. Heat shimmers. The wreck of a Land Cruiser sits on its roof, tires blown, one door off.

Under a tarp rigged from the open hatch sit three survivors.

NGAIO, fifties, a Maori geologist, cracked lips, a wide-brimmed hat held on with a scarf. TOMASZ, thirties, the driver, sunburned face, an arm in a makeshift sling. LUPE, nineteen, a student, barefoot, her boots set neatly beside her.

Ngaio unscrews a canteen. A mouthful of water for each of them. No more.

NGAIO
One sip. Hold it. Count to ten.

TOMASZ
Give me two sips. I'm bigger than the girl.

LUPE
I'm right here, Tomasz.

TOMASZ
I know you're here.

NGAIO
You get the same as the rest of us. That's the rule.

TOMASZ
Whose rule?

NGAIO
Mine. I've got the canteen.

Tomasz grunts. Takes his sip. Holds it. His eyes close.

LUPE
(whispering)
Do you think they know we're missing?

NGAIO
The lodge expects us at six tonight.

TOMASZ
They'll look tomorrow.

LUPE
That's too late, isn't it?

NGAIO
Not if we're careful with the water.

TOMASZ
And the heat.

NGAIO
And the heat. We walk at dusk. East, toward the ridge. The salt is hard there, you won't sink.

LUPE
How far?

TOMASZ
Twenty kilometers.

NGAIO
Twenty-two.

TOMASZ
Twenty-two.

LUPE
(low)
Okay. Okay.

A wind rises, spinning a small white devil of salt. It passes. The tarp snaps.

NGAIO
There. See that? Wind means weather turning. By dark, it'll cool.

TOMASZ
You say that like you've done this.

NGAIO
Thirty years of fieldwork. I've been lost on flats before.

LUPE
Were you scared?

NGAIO
Every time.

Lupe laughs, a small cracked sound. Tomasz almost smiles.

TOMASZ
Then what's the trick?

NGAIO
You stop looking at the horizon. It lies. You look at your feet and the next step.

TOMASZ
That's a lousy trick.

NGAIO
It's the only one.

LUPE
Can I try the canteen again?

NGAIO
No.

LUPE
Tomasz?

TOMASZ
She said no, Lupe.

LUPE
I'm just asking!

NGAIO
Both of you. Sit. Rest. We have eight hours.

They settle. Lupe lays her head on her knees. Tomasz studies his sling. Ngaio watches the white horizon, unmoving.

NGAIO (CONT'D)
Eight hours. Then we walk.
